description.narrative:
Democratic republic of Congo is facing a armed conflict since the last 15 years. Around 2,2 million internal displaced people(OCHA, July 2012), Most of basic socio facilities were destroyed by the war and equipments were plundered. Some IDP has returned in their place of origin, such as in Masisi. In Nord Kivu province, 41% of health facilities belong to the Catholic Church Managed directly by Caritas Goma, via le BDOM (Diocesan Medical department). Despite the effort made by humanitarian organisations to support free of charge medical care in several health Zones, Kirotche remain with very few infrastructures. This causes a high maternal mortality rate (540/100,000) due to lack of health facilities, delivery complications at home. High infant mortality rate (143/10,000). There’s a long distance between the existing health facilities and beneficiaries, more than 45 km is the distance to walk by sick people or pregnant woman to attend the Hospital. Caritas Goma made a strong advocacy to provincial authorities as well as lobbying to Caritas partners with the view of exploring ways of improving this worsen situation in which the affected population are leaving as result of war and conflicts. Caritas Goma submitted to Caritas Luxembourg this project to rehabilitate, equip, and train heath worker at Matanda health centre, in Kirotche health Zone. An agreement was therefore signed between Caritas Goma and Caritas Luxembourg for the rehabilitation. CAFOD was requested to co finance this initiative to provide Medical equipment while Caritas Goma and the Ministry of health will support training of community and heath workers. The estimated population is 19,560 people who will benefit directly from this project, most of them are internal displaced, returnees and host families. Caritas développement Goma, with the ministry of health have committed to provide health workers and their salaries. 
The overall goal of this project is to improve access to healthcare through the  rehabilitation and equipment of  Matanda health Centre, Masisi territory, in North Kivu province by the end of March 2013 so that:
•	People in targeted area have access to health facilities nearest and in timely;
•	Community health workers (30 persons)are trained and participate in improving community health status;
•	Health worker at Matanda health centre are trained on various themes selected on the basis of most diseases affecting population as well as management of public health in general.
Rehabilitation will be done by a selected tender; trainings of heath workers and community workers by ministry of health, monitoring and evaluation by a mixed team including CAFOD, Caritas Luxembourg, Caritas Goma, local community. The diocesan department of Health will be the implementation section which has a very strong experience in Primary health care management as mentioned above. As cafod funding will be used for procurement, cafod will ensure that SCM is respected as well as Caritas Goma Procurement Manual.
